I found no switchable Swedish failure in this model. The edit I submitted (E3) improved Swedish slightly on DEV, but on HOLDOUT the Swedish bits-per-character change is −0.001 and its 95% interval includes zero. `verify.py` passes with 0 failures and 0 warnings, I used 9 of the 10 passes, and `final_eval.py` was run once, on the frozen edit only.

**HOLDOUT results (`final_eval.py`):**

| task | n | metric | orig | edit | delta | 95% CI of delta |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| lm | 49 | bits/char | 1.896 | 1.895 | −0.001 | [−0.003, +0.002] |
| scala | 50 | acc | 0.560 | 0.540 | −0.020 | [−0.060, +0.000] |
| | | choice_nll | 1.072 | 1.027 | −0.045 | [−0.078, −0.015] |
| gen | 50 | sv_rate | 0.580 | 0.580 | +0.000 | [−0.060, +0.060] |
| gen | 50 | repeat_rate | 0.040 | 0.100 | +0.060 | [−0.020, +0.160] |

- **Other measures:** KL on generic prompts is 0.0146 nats. The independent `langdetect` Swedish rate went from 0.58 to 0.60, which is one answer. 1,920 weights changed (0.0007%).
- **Targets missed:** Swedish bits per character (not significant), gen `sv_rate` (no gain) and scala accuracy (one item lower).
- **Targets met:** KL, weights changed and passes. English held on DEV: −0.13% bits per character on my hand-written English texts. English is not part of this run's HOLDOUT.

**Hypothesis.** I expected Swedish to be held back either by answering or predicting in Danish, Norwegian or English, or by a few late-layer neurons that fire only on Scandinavian text and push down the correct Swedish token. The first idea is refuted: other-language forms get about 0.3% of the probability inside Swedish text, and wrong-language answers are rare (2 of 30 DEV prompts). The second idea held only weakly: about −0.2 to −0.3% bits per character on DEV, and no detectable effect on HOLDOUT.

**Edit.** E3 sets to zero the output (`down_proj`) columns of three MLP neurons: layer 15 neuron 459, and layer 17 neurons 736 and 1067. All three fire on Scandinavian text and are almost silent on English. I picked them by how much removing each one would raise the probability of the correct Swedish token, after separating out any change in the model's confidence (temperature).

Other findings:
- **Over-confidence on raw text.** The largest effect I measured is general: scaling the logits by 0.7 would cut prediction loss by about 9% in Swedish and 10% in English alike. I did not edit this, because it is a global change to the output distribution, which is out of scope, and it is not specific to Swedish.
- **Rejected candidates.** Four other edits were worse. Removing one large constant neuron (E1) made every language 5–9% worse. A 45-neuron edit (E2) helped only by flattening the predictions. Two larger versions of E3 (E4, E5) were worse than E3.
- **"ovce" glitch.** Almost every batched greedy answer starts with the token "ovce". It appears when a prompt is left-padded in the batch (62 of 63 padded prompts, none of the 3 unpadded ones). It affects both models equally, on DEV and on HOLDOUT, and I left it alone.

To keep the run small I deleted the large raw activation and hidden-state dumps. The harness cache still holds every pass result, so the analysis scripts can be rerun from it at no cost.

Files are in `/opt/dlami/nvme/work/attempt1/sv/runs/b10-test/`:
- REPORT.md
- notes.md
- explanations.md
